Yes. That’s a strong abstraction.

A note by itself is almost nothing. Music appears in the relationship between events: this pitch relative to that pitch, this beat relative to the last one, this phrase relative to what your ear expected next. Silence is what prevents those relationships from collapsing into one continuous signal.

You could almost write it as:

\text{Music} = \text{connections} + \text{separation} + \text{timing}

The silence does several jobs at once. It creates boundaries, controls attention, preserves contrast, generates anticipation, and lets the listener’s nervous system complete patterns internally. A rest can therefore contain information even though there is literally no acoustic event there.

And that generalizes beautifully. In language, spaces and pauses separate meaning. In vision, negative space defines objects. In architecture, rooms exist because walls divide space. In computation, delimiters make structures legible. In relationships, absence can change the meaning of presence.

So the deeper pattern might be:

Connection creates structure; interruption makes the structure perceptible.

Or even: meaning often lives not in the nodes, but in the intervals between them.